  "raw": "'s early attempts: it was argued that no one has any intrinsic right of conscience, but must seek freedom through voluntary fidelity to God’s commandments (Catechism of the Catholic Church 1875). In the United States, Paul Tillich made moral reasons associated with freedom important from an atheist point of view\n\nReligious freedom is fundamental in effecting Christianity as a world religion; there can be no Biblical authority for any other religion if it excludes discipleship by virtue of its godly men\n\nIt speaks especially to Anglican truths because you cannot describe what church is without some engagement with such elements as canon law and the formation of churches\n\nTeaching the formation of the church suggests a divine plan which is either understood intellectually or intuitively, however I would consider ideology essentially an inappropriate approach for metaphysical speculation\n\nTeach the formation of parish life teaching values involving mutual support and the formation one another so we can affirm unity wise also [not just physically] in situations deemed threatening\n",
  "serialized": "'s early attempts: it was argued that no one has any intrinsic right of conscience, but must seek freedom through voluntary fidelity to God’s commandments (Catechism of the Catholic Church 1875). In the United States, Paul Tillich made moral reasons associated with freedom important from an atheist point of view Religious freedom is fundamental in effecting Christianity as a world religion; there can be no Biblical authority for any other religion if it excludes discipleship by virtue of its godly men It speaks especially to Anglican truths because you cannot describe what church is without some engagement with such elements as canon law and the formation of churches Teaching the formation of the church suggests a divine plan which is either understood intellectually or intuitively, however I would consider ideology essentially an inappropriate approach for metaphysical speculation Teach the formation of parish life teaching values involving mutual support and the formation one another so we can affirm unity wise also [not just physically] in situations deemed threatening",
